\description{
Prints the two tables that describe how cleanly the model assigns cases.

The \strong{Average Posterior Probability (AvePP)} matrix has one row per set of
observations modally assigned to a class and one column per class, holding
the mean posterior probability. High values on the diagonal, low values off
it, indicate well-separated classes.

The \strong{classification table} cross-classifies the probabilistic memberships
against the modal assignment, and yields the classification error: the
proportion of cases the modal rule is expected to place in the wrong class.
See \code{\link[=classification_table]{classification_table()}} for the details, and \code{\link[=absolute_fit]{absolute_fit()}} and
\code{\link[=bivariate_residuals]{bivariate_residuals()}} for the fit of the model itself rather than the
quality of its assignments.

The diagonal of the AvePP matrix reads as a percentage of the cases assigned
to that class: a value of 0.91 "suggests that 91\% of subjects in the assigned
class fit that category, while 9\% of the subjects in that class do not
accurately fit that category" (Fanti & Henrich, 2010, as reported by Lee et
al., 2023, p. 653).

This function does not compute entropy, which is the other number solutions
are judged by. That is \code{fit$metrics$entropy}, printed as \verb{Rel. Entropy} and
carried as the \code{Entropy} column of \code{\link[=compare_mixtures]{compare_mixtures()}} and
\code{\link[=compare_longitudinal]{compare_longitudinal()}}, where it is documented.

Both tables use the case weights when the model was fitted with any.

Three further blocks print when they apply. None carries a threshold, a
warning or a verdict; each is a measurement to report and judge.

\strong{Class separation} (all-continuous models only): the Mahalanobis distance
between each pair of class means, against the pooled within-class variance
of each indicator. It is a property of the parameters on the indicators' own
scale, not of how cleanly cases were assigned, which is what entropy
measures; with one indicator it is Cohen's d. The smallest distance is the
one that limits class enumeration: Tein, Coxe and Cham (2013) found
"little power to detect the correct number of classes for small (d = .2) or
even medium (d = .5) inter-class distances, under any conditions or with any
selection methods", and that sample size mattered far less than separation.
Read it in one direction only. When the true classes are poorly separated
the fitted classes are not the true ones, and their distance overstates the
truth: in a simulation of three classes at true neighbouring distance 1
(n = 600) the reported smallest distance averaged 1.6, while at true
distance 3 it averaged 2.9. A small value says the classes are close; a
moderate one does not by itself show they are not. For categorical
indicators no distance is reported, since Cohen's d does not exist there.

\strong{Modal minus probability-weighted class means} (continuous and binary
indicators; a binary mean is the proportion endorsing): each class's
indicator means computed over the cases modally assigned to it, minus the
same means weighted by the posterior probabilities of the measurement model.
It shows how much exporting a modal class variable and analysing it as if it
were known changes what the classes look like (Chen et al., 2026). The
package's own answer to classification error is to correct for it, with
\code{\link[=add_covariates]{add_covariates()}} and \code{\link[=add_outcome]{add_outcome()}}; this block is for the analysis
that will use the exported variable anyway.

\strong{Cases moved by the third step} (a structural model attached with
\code{correction = "ML"}): the modal class under the measurement model's
posterior against the modal class under the step-3 posterior, as a count,
a proportion and a cross-tabulation. The three-step method assumes the
structural model leaves the classes as step 1 defined them (Vermunt, 2010;
Asparouhov & Muthén, 2014); many moved cases mean the structural model is redefining the
classes, and the one-step and two-step fits are the comparison to run.
